53/51 | fences | Rails stacked as breastworks by Buford's troopers, taken from a fence on the eastern edge of a wheatfield between the railroad grading and the turnpike | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says a squad of a Pennsylvania regiment took advantage of a pile of rails stacked as breastworks by Buford's troopers, which were carried from a fence bounding the eastern edge of a wheatfield that sloped westward down the ridge between the railroad grading and the turnpike | =Here the squad took advantage of a pile of rails stacked as breastworks by Buford's troopers. The rails were carried from a fence bounding the eastern edge of a wheatfield which sloped westward down the ridge between the railroad grading and the turnpike.
43/41 | fields | A high wheatfield north of the railroad grading on July 1 | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says that men of Davis's captured brigade escaped through the railroad cut toward the Confederate lines through a high wheatfield north of the railroad grading | =through a high wheatfield north of the railroad grading.
63/61 | fields | Confederates crawled on hands and knees through the high wheat to surprise a color guard | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says a squad of Confederates crawled on their hands and knees through the high wheat to escape detection and surprised the color guard of a Pennsylvania regiment | =These Confederates had crawled on their hands and knees through the high wheat to escape detection,
57/55 | fences | A post and rail fence paralleling the south side of the Cashtown Pike | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says Pennsylvania companies lay behind the post and rail fence paralleling the south side of the pike | =behind the post and rail fence paralleling the south side of the pike.
67/65 | fields | The cornfields and orchard of the McPherson acreage | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says that when the last of Stone's Brigade had retreated toward the seminary the McPherson acreage saw no more hostilities over its cornfields and orchard | =When the last of Stone's Brigade melted back towards the seminary, and eventually into town and the Cemetery heights, McPherson's acreage saw no more hostilities over its cornfields and orchard.
33/31 | woodlots | Herbst Woods, the woodlot of the 160-acre Herbst farm, was wholly Herbst's; the McPherson woodlot lay north of the Cashtown Pike east of the Wills farm | bld-herbst | 1863-07 | stated | The study says the entire length and width of the grove called Reynolds Woods or McPherson Woods was owned by John Herbst at the time of the battle and was the woodlot portion of his 160-acre farm, that the McPherson woodlot was north of the Cashtown Pike and east of the James Wills farm, and that Edward McPherson never owned the Herbst Woods | =But the fact remains that the entire length and width of the grove was owned by John Herbst at the time of the Battle of Gettysburg, and was the woodlot portion of the 160-acre Herbst Farm. The McPherson woodlot, at that time, was north of the Cashtown Pike and east of the James Wills Farm (in the area now erroneously called Sheads' Woods). The Herbst Woods were never owned by Edward McPherson, John B. {{McPheison|McPherson}}, or any other McPherson from the time antecedent to the battle to the {{tine|time}} after the battle.
33/31 | buildings | The Herbst barn was burned because Union soldiers sniped from his buildings; the McPherson buildings were not lost | bld-herbst,bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says John Herbst had his barn burned because Union soldiers used his buildings to snipe at Heth's men, and that McPherson lost no buildings | =Poor John Herbst had his barn burned because Yankee soldiers used his {{buildirgs|buildings}} from which to snipe at Heth's men. (McPherson lost no buildings.)
70/- | woodlots | A large white oak just north of Herbst Woods and the old fence line, about one third its present size in 1863 | bld-herbst | 1863-07-01 (the tree as the study reconstructs its size) | hedged | The study says a large white oak just north of Herbst Woods and the old fence line, with a circumference of over nine feet now and an age of about 170 years, would have been about one third of that size in 1863 | =A large white oak, just north of Herbst Woods and the old fence line, stands at fifty yards east of the 7th Wisconsin monument. This tree, with a circumference of over nine feet now, is approximately 170 years old, and is apparently the only old tree in this part of the field north of the woods. It would have been about one-third that size in 1863
%basis Lieutenant Colonel J. A. Walker, 45th Georgia, "Some Stirring Incidents", Philadelphia Times, March 17, 188-, quoted by the study (footnote 105)
73/71 | fences | Wounded sitting about the fence corners; the fence at the corner of Reynolds woods where it turns toward Herr's Tavern | - | 1863-07-01 (evening; an account of the 1880s) | stated | The study quotes a Confederate officer's account that after burying many in the field he came about sundown to the corner of the Reynolds woods and rested on a fence very near the corner where it turns toward Herr's Tavern, and that a few wounded sat about the fence corners | =I first came upon the ground fought over by McGowan's South Carolina troops. Many of his dead were still unburied and a few wounded sitting about the fence {{comers|corners}}. . . . We buried a great many in this field and about sundown came to the corner of the Reynolds woods, where General Reynolds had been killed that day. I rested for a while on this fence, very near the {{comer|corner}} where it turns towards Herr's Tavern.
%basis -
73/71 | hospitals | July 1: the ambulatory wounded sought refuge in the McPherson farmhouse and barn; the fields strewn with the dead and wounded | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study says that the wounded who were ambulatory enough sought refuge in the McPherson farmhouse and barn and that at dusk on July 1 the fields around the farm were strewn with the dead, dying and wounded of both armies | =Those wounded who were ambulatory enough to make it, sought refuge in the McPherson farmhouse and {{bam|barn}}, while those less fortunate in the serious nature of their wounds had to rely on the devotion and bravery of friends to assist them in reaching even the crudest of shelter (such as in Major Chamberlin's case). As dusk closed in on July 1, however, the fields around the McPherson Farm were strewn with the dead, dying, and wounded men of both armies,
%basis Lieutenant Colonel J. A. Walker, 45th Georgia, as quoted by the study (footnote 105)
74/72 | hospitals | The tenant house, taken and retaken, riddled and filled with dead and wounded Federals but not materially damaged | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study quotes a Confederate officer's account that the farm house, a few hundred yards north of the Reynolds oak, was taken and retaken, riddled by bullets and filled with the dead and dying, the cows and horses shot down by stray bullets, and yet not materially damaged, and that he found it filled with Federal dead and wounded | =Taken and re-taken, riddled by bullets, filled with dead and dying, the very cows and horses shot down by stray bullets, and yet not materially damaged. Seeing a light in it I went to see if my services were needed, I found it filled with Federal dead and wounded.
%basis Lieutenant R. B. Beath, 88th Pennsylvania, as quoted by the study
76/74 | hospitals | The McPherson barn full of bleeding and mangled soldiers without a surgeon after July 1 | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | The study quotes a Union lieutenant's account that on reaching McPherson's barn he found it full of bleeding and mangled soldiers in a most distressed and sickening condition, without a surgeon to dress their festering wounds and bind up their splintered bones | =upon reaching McPherson's {{bam|barn}} he found it full of bleeding and mangled soldiers in a most distressed and sickening condition, without a surgeon to dress their festering wounds and bind up their splintered bones.
%basis -
78/76 | hospitals | After the armies withdrew the wounded in and about the McPherson farm buildings were unattended until citizens came | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-04 to 1863-07-05 | stated | The study says that after the withdrawal of the armies the soldiers still lay in and about McPherson's farm buildings unattended, that it was not until the Confederate forces melted back toward the Potomac that those in the dark barn saw hope, and that the first to relieve them were concerned citizens, not army or civil surgeons | =After the withdrawal of the armies starting on July {{5>|5}}, these soldiers still lay in and about McPherson's farm buildings unattended. | =It was not until after the Confederate forces melted back towards the Potomac that those lying within the dark {{bam|barn}} saw any rays of hope. The first to relieve the wants of these melancholy soldiers were not army or civil surgeons, but concerned citizens from the area.
%basis a civilian's account (probably William McClean, "The Days of Terror in 1863") as quoted by the study (footnote 110)
79/77 | hospitals | The McPherson barn crowded with the wounded of both armies, some four days without food or surgical attention | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-05 | stated | The study quotes a civilian's account that on entering the barn on the Chambersburg Pike he found it crowded with the wounded of both armies, some having fallen four days before, without food or surgical attention, so closely packed that he had to tramp on some of them while handing out supplies | =When I entered the barn it was crowded with the wounded of both armies, some of them having fallen four days before and without having any food, except in some cases the little hardtack in their haversacks, and without any surgical attention to their wounds. There was so many of these wounded and so closely packed together, that I was obliged to tramp on some of them in distributing my supplies.
%basis a chaplain's account of July 5 quoted by the study
80/78 | hospitals | July 5: the barn above and below, the wagon shed, the tenant house, the pig sty and the barnyard crowded with badly wounded soldiers | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-05 | stated | The study quotes a chaplain's account of the day after the armies withdrew that the barn above and below, the wagon shed, the tenant house, the pig sty and the open barnyard were all crowded with badly wounded soldiers, and that not until about noon did part of the hospital corps arrive, only one surgeon and two assistants | =When I examined the situation I found that the barn above and below, the wagon shed, the tenant house, the pig sty, and the open barnyard were all crowded with badly wounded soldiers. | =As the time passed, other persons strolled in and rendered aid, but it was not until about noon that any part of the hospital corps arrived. And then only one surgeon and two assistants came to render their service.

# Chapters VI-VII of the study: the losses of the tenant, the hospital use of house and barn, and what the barn and the house looked like in 1863 against 1895 and 1905.
# PDF pp. 83, 84, 85, 89 and 90 were looked at as page images (printed pages 81, 82, 83, 87, 88); the text layer reads 1905 as 1903 on p. 89 and 1904 as 190U on p. 84.
%images 83, 84, 85, 89, 90
%basis the tenant's damage claim, as summed up by the study (footnote 112 cites Gardner, "The Carnage at Gettysburg", Civil War Times 1961)
81/79 | damage | The tenant's losses on the McPherson farm: four horses, three cows, five calves, four hogs, forty chickens, three turkeys and four tons of hay in the barn | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The study says an inventory of the damage showed the tenant family lost four horses, three cows and five calves, four hogs, at least forty chickens and three turkeys, and that four tons of hay stored in the barn were destroyed, used as bedding for the wounded or forage for the hundreds of horses encamped on the farm for five days | =A cursory inventory of the damage revealed that the tenant family lost their four horses (one bay mare, one black mare, a yearling colt, and another bay horse) as well as three cows and five calves. Lesser stock loss included four hogs, at least forty chickens, and three turkeys. Four tons of hay stored in the {{bam|barn}} were destroyed--either for use as bedding for the wounded or for forage for the hundreds of horses encamped on the farm for five days.
%basis Sarah Slentz, "Local Woman Fled with Mother to Seminary Here" (a newspaper article; the study's footnotes 114 and 116)
83/81 | hospitals | Over 200 soldiers were cared for in the McPherson house and barn alone; two soldiers were buried in the garden; the house was unfit for three months | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The study says that it was three months until the house was fit again, that according to Sarah Slentz over 200 soldiers were cared for in the house and barn alone until they could be moved to other field hospitals, and that on returning the tenants found two soldiers buried in the garden adjoining the house | =It was three months until the house was fit and furnished again for occupancy by the Slentzes. During that time the family stayed at the seminary and with John Slentz, Sr. According to Sarah Slentz, over 200 soldiers were cared for in the house and barn alone until they could be transported to other field hospitals. On their return to their home, the tenants found two soldiers buried in the garden adjoining the house, one of them wrapped in a quilt belonging to the family.
83/81 | damage | The 1863 Brady photograph shows no severe battle damage on the south or west sides of the McPherson buildings; the house was said to be scarred by bullets and shells | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The study says that the 1863 Brady photograph does not indicate any severe battle damage on the south or west sides of the buildings, although the owner called them in a state of great dilapidation and the Slentzes said the stone and log house was battle scarred and marked by bullets and shells on the exterior | =It is odd that he did not file for battle damages to the house and barn, especially since he commented that they were in a state of "great dilapidation" (although the 1863 Brady photograph does not indicate any severe battle damage on the south or west sides of the buildings).
%basis -
84/82 | buildings | By 1904 the only structure of the war-time period still standing on the McPherson farm was the stone barn | bld-mcpherson-farm | ~1904 (condition when the government acquired the farm buildings; the buildings of 1863 other than the barn were gone) | stated | The study says that through the land transfers from 1868 until the United States acquired the McPherson farm buildings in 1904 the only structure remaining of the war-time period was the stone barn | =Unfortunately, through all the myriad of land transfers from 1868 until the U.S. Government acquired the McPherson farm buildings in {{I 90U|1904}}, {{117|}} the only structure remaining of the war-time period was the stone barn.
85/83 | buildings | The farmhouse burned in 1895; its kitchen was the original log part; a two-story frame house stood where the old stone house had stood | bld-mcpherson-farm | !1895 (fire; the frame house replaced the stone house at a date after 1863 that the study does not give) | stated | The study says a fire on April 6, 1895 consumed the whole house, that the kitchen section was the original log part of the farmhouse, and that it adjoined a two-story yellow frame house on its northern gable end where the old stone house originally stood, on the same foundation as the stone structure it replaced | =On April 6, {{189^|1895}}, a fire, caused by a faulty flue in the kitchen part of the house, ignited the roof and consumed the entire structure (as well, apparently, as a nearby shed). The kitchen section of the house was the original log part of the Breadon-McConaughy-{{ClaxksonMcPherson|Clarkson-McPherson}} farmhouse. This log kitchen adjoined a two-story {{yellcw|yellow}} frame house on its northern gable end--where the old stone house originally stood.
85/83 | buildings | The log kitchen of 1895 was the log kitchen of 1863; the exterior chimney on its south gable end appears the same in the 1863 photograph | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 | hedged | The study says it is relatively certain from photographs of the 1880s-1895 that the kitchen section was the same log kitchen of 1863 and not the stone house, since the location and similarity of the exterior chimney on the south gable end of the kitchen appear the same in the 1863 photograph as in the later photographs | =it is relatively certain from an examination of {{I880s-l895|1880s-1895}} photographs that the kitchen section was the self-same log kitchen of 1863, and not the stone house. The location and similarity of the exterior chimney on the south gable end of the kitchen appears the same in the 1863 photo as it does in the later-period photos of the two-story frame and adjoining one-story unit.
87/85 | buildings | The outbuildings that complemented the barn in 1863-1895 are gone | bld-mcpherson-farm | ~1970s (condition at the time of the study; the outbuildings stood in 1863-1895) | stated | The study says any outbuildings which complemented the barn in 1863-1895 are now gone | Any outbuildings which complemented the barn in 1863-1895 are now gone.
89/87 | buildings | The west-side lean-to sheds of the McPherson barn appear to be those shown in the 1863 Brady stereo view | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 | hedged | The study says the lean-to sheds on the west side of the barn, torn off and rebuilt in the barn's repair, appear from photographs to be the same lean-tos that show in the 1863 Brady stereo view | =What Irwin did was to tear off the falling lean-to sheds on the barn's west side. These sheds appear from photos to be the same lean-tos that show in the 1863 Brady stereo view.
89/87 | buildings | The north-end lean-to of the barn was added sometime after the battle | bld-mcpherson-farm | !after 1863 (added after the battle; torn off in 1905) | stated | The study says the north-end lean-to of the barn, which Irwin tore off, was added sometime after the battle | =He also tore off the north-end lean-to, which was added sometime after the battle.
89/87 | buildings | The south end wall of the barn was originally stone from foundation to gable; by the late 1880s the south gable was vertical siding with pigeon holes | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863 and earlier (the original stone end wall); the vertical siding of the late 1880s is later | stated | The study says the barn's end wall was originally stone from foundation to gable and that by the late 1880s the south gable was vertical siding with pigeon holes cut into it, plausibly because the gable wall had collapsed or threatened to | =The end wall was originally stone from foundation to gable, but by the late 1880s the south gable was of vertical siding with pigeon holes cut into the siding at intervals. It is plausible that the gable wall had collapsed or had threatened to collapse and was replaced by the vertical siding.
89/87 | buildings | In 1905 the west lean-to bays were rebuilt about ten feet shorter than before | bld-mcpherson-farm | !1905 (rebuilding; the bays of today are shorter than those of 1863) | stated | The study says that when the west-end lean-to bays were put back in 1905 their length was foreshortened by about ten feet, a facsimile construction and not a preservation job | =When he put back the west-end lean-to bays he foreshortened their length by about ten feet, so that what he did was not a preservation job, but a "facsimile" construction. {{Binoe|Since}} this {{1903|1905}} non-historical replacement by Irwin, the War Department and National Park Service have been continuing to preserve the foreshortened bays.
90/88 | buildings | The south stone wall of the barn had embrasures, not put back when the wall was rebuilt in 1905 | bld-mcpherson-farm | ~1905 (the wall as found in 1905; the study does not date the embrasures) | stated | The study says that in repairing the cracked south wall the mason tore down the center section, rebuilt the wall to just about the eaves, where it met the vertical siding of the 1880s, and did not put back the embrasures that were there | =and rebuilt the wall to just about the eaves, where it met the 1880s vertical siding. He did not put back the embrasures that were there. | @89: =Irwin, in order to repair the crack and bulge, tore down only the center section of the stone wall
90/88 | buildings | The barn's north gable had a plastered cartouche with a datestone, deteriorated by 1903 and not replaced; a frame wagon shed was attached to the north end | bld-mcpherson-farm | pre-1863 (original barn fabric; the cartouche had deteriorated by 1903) | stated | The study says that after tearing away the frame wagon shed attached to the north end of the barn the mason repointed the north gable wall but did not replace the plastered cartouche, which had deteriorated by 1903, and calls it the historic datestone cartouche | =In addition, after tearing away the frame wagon shed attached to the north end of the {{bam|barn}}, Irwin repointed the north gable wall, but did not replace the plastered cartouche which had been deteriorated by 1903.
90/88 | buildings | In 1905 the lean-to bays were shortened, the roof line revised and the double-hung wagon doors moved | bld-mcpherson-farm | !1905 (alterations of the rebuilding) | stated | The study says that everything the mason touched became an alteration, the foreshortened lean-to bays, the embrasureless south wall and a north gable without its historic datestone cartouche, and that by foreshortening the bays he revised the roof line and changed the location of the double-hung wagon doors | =Everything Irwin touched had become an alteration--the foreshortened lean-to bays, the embrasureless south wall, and a north gable without its historic datestone cartouche. In addition, by foreshortening the lean-to bays, he also revised the roof-line of the barn, going so far as to changed the location of the double-hung wagon doors.

