# Appendix B of the study: the claim file of the landlord Edward McPherson (Pennsylvania damage claims; filed 1868, never paid), transcribed by the study. PDF pp. 106 and 107 were looked at as page images (printed pp. 104 and 105).
# The text layer of these two pages is letter-spaced and misreads digits (6844 as 681*1*, 547.52 as 51*7.52, the fraction one quarter as a backslash, 4th as i t t h); each correction is marked {{saved|fixed}} and was checked on the image.
%images 106, 107
%basis the claim file of Edward McPherson, Appendix B of the study (the appraisement of July 22, 1863 and the affidavits of January 1883)
106/104 | fences | The landlord's claim for the fences destroyed July 1-4, 1863: 6,844 rails, 800 feet of boards and 138 posts, appraised at $581.60 | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 to 1863-07-04 (appraised July 22, 1863) | stated | The study transcribes the claim of Edward McPherson for the fences destroyed July 1-4, 1863, appraised on July 22, 1863 by three neighbors as 6,844 rails at 8 cents, 800 feet of boards and 138 posts at 16 cents, $581.60 in all | =Ex 2 Appraisement {{681*1*|6844}} Rails @8 800 feet Boards 138 Posts 16 {{51*7.52|547.52}} 12.00 22.08 1.50 $581.60 July 22/63 John Hoover, E. Hannaway and Abraham Spangler swear that the above {{apprasement|apprasement}} is correct according to their best judgment | =For {{1 s t t o l* th|1st to 4th}} July 1863
%basis the affidavit of John T. Slentz, the tenant, sworn January 8, 1883, in the landlord's claim file (Appendix B of the study)
106/104 | fences | Buford's command occupied the farm until 10 a.m. on July 1 and cut and threw down about a quarter mile of post and rail fence to make way for the battle | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-06-30 to 1863-07-01 (account sworn January 1883) | stated | The study transcribes the tenant's affidavit that the battle began and General Reynolds was killed on the landlord's farm, that the day before the battle General Buford's command occupied the place until 10 a.m. next day and cut and threw down about a quarter mile of post and rail fence to make way for the battle | =That battle commenced and Gen'l Reynolds was killed on Claimant's farm. The day before the battle Genl Buford's command occupied the place until 10 am next day, they cut and threw down about {{\|¼}} mile post and rail fence to make way for the battle.
107/105 | fences | About a cord of the rails used as fuel; after the battle the thrown-down fencing and many rails lying in the ground; many rails used to burn dead horses | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 to 1863-07-04 (account sworn January 1883) | stated | The study transcribes the tenant's affidavit that he saw about one cord of the rails used for fuel, that the rebels occupied the place from July 1 to 4, that after the battle he saw where the fencing had been thrown down and a great many rails in the ground, and that after they left many rails were used to burn up dead horses | =Saw about 1 cord of the rails used for fuel. The Rebels occupied the place from the 1st to July {{i t t h|4th}}. After the battle was over, Affirmant saw where the fencing had been thrown down, and a great many rails were in the ground. Did not see what the Rebels did when they were on the {{place j|place;}} after they left many rails were used to {{b u m|burn}} up dead horses.
107/105 | fences | A board fence one eighth of a mile long, boards five to six inches wide, one board fence gone after the battle; the posts belonged to it and to the post and rail fence | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 to 1863-07-04 (account sworn January 1883) | stated | The study transcribes the tenant's affidavit that there was a board fence one eighth of a mile long, with 5 to 6 inch boards to the panel, whose boards were all gone after the battle, and that he supposes the posts belonged to this fence and to the post and rail fence | =There was a board fence 1/8 mile long 5--6 inch boards to the panel{{j|;}} the boards were all gone after the battle. Supposes the posts belonged to this and the post and rail fence.
%basis the statement of Abraham Spangler, the landlord's agent (now deceased in 1883), in the landlord's claim file (Appendix B of the study)
107/105 | fences | The fencing of the McPherson farm: board fences on both sides of the lane to the house, a picket fence round the garden and yard, a portion of post fence, the larger part worm fence; division fences not much disturbed | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07 (account of the landlord's agent of 1883) | stated | The study transcribes the statement of the landlord's agent that the fencing on both sides of the road was torn down and pretty consumed, that to the south and north the farm was generally one field deep and the division fences were not much disturbed, that there was a board fence on both sides of the lane to the house and a picket fence around the garden and yard, and that a portion of the fencing was post but the larger part was worm fence | =The fencing on both sides the road was torn down and pretty consumed. On the south and north the farm was generally one field deep, does not think the division fences were much disturbed. There was a board fence running on both sides of the lane to the house and a picket fence around the garden and yard. Portion of the fencing was post, but the larger part was worm fence. | =Abraham Spangler now dec'd was his Agent.
